  user_input {
    user_response: "Act as a Principal Full-Stack Software Engineer. I want to build a mega-app called \"DP Place Profiles\" for IB Geography case-study analysis. \n\nGenerate the complete, clean, modular code for a responsive dashboard application using React, Tailwind CSS, Lucide React icons, and a charting framework (like Recharts or standard CSS/SVG vectors for maximum standalone compatibility).\n\nThe application must include the following functional layout and UI states:\n\n1. TOP NAVIGATION & CONTROLS:\n   - A country selection dropdown and an interactive time-series timeline slider (spanning 1970 to 2026/2040) that controls the global state for the population visualizations.\n\n2. GLOBALISATION TAB:\n   - Displays KOF Index dimensions (Economic, Social, Political) broken into distinct visual progress bars or meters comparing \"De Facto\" vs. \"De Jure\" scores.\n   - Separate status widgets for A.T. Kearney Global Cities Index integration and historical EY index placeholders.\n\n3. ECONOMY TAB:\n   - A sector profile layout displaying % employed in Primary, Secondary, Tertiary, and Quaternary sectors. \n   - A toggle switch labeled \"Group Service/Knowledge Sectors\" that visually merges Tertiary and Quaternary into a single block or explodes them apart.\n   - Color styling rule: Primary must use an amber/earth tone; Secondary, Tertiary, and Quaternary must use varying saturation shades of the exact same blue/cobalt color family.\n   - Structural panels showing Top Imports/Exports and Top Bilateral Trade Partners.\n\n4. HUMAN GEOGRAPHY & SYSTEM CONNECTIONS TAB:\n   - Grid cards mapping out sub-national spatial hubs (SEZs/EPZs), tourism complexes, and core-periphery dynamics.\n   - A dedicated \"Political Economy Ledger\" table tracking Informal Economy % of GDP, EIU Governance Classification, Freedom House Status, and Transparency International CPI scores.\n\n5. \'PRISONERS OF GEOGRAPHY\' PHYISCAL LAYER:\n   - A stylized visual layout mapping Topographic Friction Points, Hydrological Arteries, Choke Points, and Geopolitical Buffer Zones with rich contextual analytical descriptions.\n\n6. POPULATION DYNAMICS TAB (THE ENGINE):\n   - A dual-pane split-screen synchronized directly to the main timeline slider.\n   - Left Pane: An interactive Population Pyramid displaying 5-year age-gender cohorts (Male/Female).\n   - Right Pane: A sub-national Admin-1 level (provinces/states) choropleth map layout rendering population density gradients that change dynamically as the slider moves.\n   - A dynamic text widget printing out a synoptic evaluation of the changing population structure.\n\nProvide all necessary component code, layout structure, tailwind styles, and robust mock JSON data directly within the application state for a primary sample country (like Bangladesh or Vietnam) so the app runs instantly and beautifully."
    items {
      text: "Act as a Principal Full-Stack Software Engineer. I want to build a mega-app called \"DP Place Profiles\" for IB Geography case-study analysis. \n\nGenerate the complete, clean, modular code for a responsive dashboard application using React, Tailwind CSS, Lucide React icons, and a charting framework (like Recharts or standard CSS/SVG vectors for maximum standalone compatibility).\n\nThe application must include the following functional layout and UI states:\n\n1. TOP NAVIGATION & CONTROLS:\n   - A country selection dropdown and an interactive time-series timeline slider (spanning 1970 to 2026/2040) that controls the global state for the population visualizations.\n\n2. GLOBALISATION TAB:\n   - Displays KOF Index dimensions (Economic, Social, Political) broken into distinct visual progress bars or meters comparing \"De Facto\" vs. \"De Jure\" scores.\n   - Separate status widgets for A.T. Kearney Global Cities Index integration and historical EY index placeholders.\n\n3. ECONOMY TAB:\n   - A sector profile layout displaying % employed in Primary, Secondary, Tertiary, and Quaternary sectors. \n   - A toggle switch labeled \"Group Service/Knowledge Sectors\" that visually merges Tertiary and Quaternary into a single block or explodes them apart.\n   - Color styling rule: Primary must use an amber/earth tone; Secondary, Tertiary, and Quaternary must use varying saturation shades of the exact same blue/cobalt color family.\n   - Structural panels showing Top Imports/Exports and Top Bilateral Trade Partners.\n\n4. HUMAN GEOGRAPHY & SYSTEM CONNECTIONS TAB:\n   - Grid cards mapping out sub-national spatial hubs (SEZs/EPZs), tourism complexes, and core-periphery dynamics.\n   - A dedicated \"Political Economy Ledger\" table tracking Informal Economy % of GDP, EIU Governance Classification, Freedom House Status, and Transparency International CPI scores.\n\n5. \'PRISONERS OF GEOGRAPHY\' PHYISCAL LAYER:\n   - A stylized visual layout mapping Topographic Friction Points, Hydrological Arteries, Choke Points, and Geopolitical Buffer Zones with rich contextual analytical descriptions.\n\n6. POPULATION DYNAMICS TAB (THE ENGINE):\n   - A dual-pane split-screen synchronized directly to the main timeline slider.\n   - Left Pane: An interactive Population Pyramid displaying 5-year age-gender cohorts (Male/Female).\n   - Right Pane: A sub-national Admin-1 level (provinces/states) choropleth map layout rendering population density gradients that change dynamically as the slider moves.\n   - A dynamic text widget printing out a synoptic evaluation of the changing population structure.\n\nProvide all necessary component code, layout structure, tailwind styles, and robust mock JSON data directly within the application state for a primary sample country (like Bangladesh or Vietnam) so the app runs instantly and beautifully."
    }
  }
}
