  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Considering User Request**\n\nI\'m focusing on the user\'s explicit instructions. They propose leveraging populationpyramid.net for population dynamics. This implies data embedding or direct usage. The second aspect is straightforward: they desire comprehensive country profiles. I am now evaluating the scope and feasibility of these profile creations.\n\n\n**Addressing Feasibility Constraints**\n\nI\'ve realized that creating profiles for every country isn\'t practical given the extensive detail requested. Generating mock data for ~195 countries exceeds current constraints and time. I intend to explain this limitation. To demonstrate the scalability of the architecture I will include a few more key case study countries such as China, Nigeria, USA, or Germany, in the code and provide instructions on how the user may add more. I am focusing on showing the user how to integrate new data, rather than generating all the data, which seems feasible.\n\n\n**Expanding Country Profiles**\n\nI\'m now expanding the country data. I\'ve decided to add China and Nigeria as examples to `src/data.ts`, enriching the existing framework. I\'m focusing on concise, key indicators for these additions, and updating the dropdown in `App.tsx` to handle dynamic country selection. I\'ll include a link to populationpyramid.net in the PopulationTab, or possibly an iframe, depending on CSP compliance.\n\n\n**Integrating Data and Links**\n\nI\'m now incorporating specific data for China, including primary, secondary, tertiary, and quaternary sector percentages, and top export information, following a similar structure to the existing Bangladesh data. I\'m also updating `src/App.tsx` to include China and Nigeria profiles, and modifying the country selection dropdown. Finally, I\'m working on embedding a link to populationpyramid.net within the PopulationTab, directly referencing the user-selected country and year for a dynamic population pyramid display.\n\n\n**Constructing Country Profiles**\n\nI\'m now building the `chinaProfile` and `nigeriaProfile` objects, adding the specific data structures for each. This includes country metadata, income classification, HDI, and globalization indicators. I\'m focusing on incorporating the relevant data within the specified data structure for these two countries as part of expanding the app.\n\n\n**Adding China Data**\n\nI\'m now integrating the provided economic data for China. Specifically, I am focusing on the employment structure percentages and the main export information to include in the country profile. I am also working on formatting the data into the appropriate data structure. I will add this to the code now.\n\n\n**Incorporating Trade Data**\n\nI\'m now integrating detailed trade data for China into the profile. This includes both outgoing and incoming trade partner information, specifying values in USD billions. I am also planning to include this information for Nigeria as well. I\'m focusing on structuring this data in a readable manner, for inclusion in the profile for both countries.\n\n\n\n**Analyzing Geopolitical Dynamics**\n\nI\'m now diving into China\'s geopolitical context. I\'ve begun analyzing the core-periphery dynamics, specifically the contrasting economic activities of the coastal regions versus the western provinces experiencing out-migration. This informs the broader understanding of regional disparities. I am focusing on documenting these key points for integration.\n\nI\'m now incorporating detailed trade data for China into the profile. This includes both outgoing and incoming trade partner information, specifying values in USD billions. I am also planning to include this information for Nigeria as well. I\'m focusing on structuring this data in a readable manner, for inclusion in the profile for both countries.\n\n\n**Adding Historical Context**\n\nI\'m now integrating historical context. I\'m focusing on adding the \"Historical buffer against Northern steppe incursions