    thinking: "**Analyzing CORS Implementation**\n\nI\'ve determined that CORS isn\'t enabled because the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header is absent. Consequently, direct client-side fetching is blocked. I\'m now exploring the implications of this restriction and how to work around or implement CORS for broader accessibility.\n\n\n**Considering Proxy and Build**\n\nI\'m now focusing on implementation. Initially, the Express proxy seemed viable, especially with Vite in development. However, I\'ve realized its limitations in a static GitHub Pages deployment, which has me rethinking the optimal approach. Instead, I\'m now leaning towards fetching and pre-baking the data into JSON files at build time within the `public/` directory. This bypasses CORS and ensures data availability. With 1065 requests needed (15 countries * 71 years), a Node script to pre-fetch seems reasonable. I\'m exploring fetching all years and compressing/saving it into a single JSON file.\n\n\n"
