    step_generation_version: 1
    model_info {
      model_name: "gemini-pro-latest"
      thinking_level: 3
    }
    started_at {
      seconds: 1781163803
      nanos: 236028088
    }
  }
  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Gender Percentage Shift**\n\nI\'ve been looking at the gender percentages and noted that the original implementation had `male_pct: 9.15` and `female_pct: 8.87`. Adding `stackId=\"stack\"` seemed to introduce issues with the male values. It seems negative male values could be the key, and Recharts `stackOffset=\"sign\"` handles those splits correctly. But, there\'s a `.reverse()` in the code, which could be relevant.\n\n\n**Addressing the \"No Males\" Issue**\n\nI\'ve explored the \"no males\" issue, tracing it to the array reversal to fix the upside-down pyramid. Reversing the pyramid structure with `[...activeNode.pyramid_structure.cohorts].reverse()` was the key fix. I considered negative values in Recharts vertical bar charts. The XAxis type is correctly set to \"number,\" and YAxis to \"category.\" Clipping the XAxis domain was also considered as a culprit. Then I realized stacking may be unnecessary: with one negative series and one positive series, `stackOffset=\"sign\"` may not be needed at all, simplifying the approach.\n\n\n**Considering Chart Splitting**\n\nI\'m now thinking about how to render the data in Recharts. Initially, I considered stacking. However, I realized Recharts\' default vertical rendering of grouped bars within a category doesn\'t work for the pyramid. I then considered using different `stackId`s, but that wouldn\'t resolve the side-by-side presentation. So, the best solution might be to use two separate `BarChart`s, one for males and one for females, using a reverse XAxis for the male chart. Many implementations do it that way. I\'ll look into a Recharts Population Pyramid example for any further ideas.\n\n\n**Clarifying StackId\'s Behavior**\n\nI\'ve realized that using the same `stackId` in Recharts sums the male and female values. When `male` is negative (-9) and `female` is positive (+8), the sum is -1. This explains why the chart looks broken: the positive bar starts at -9 and ends at -1, skewing it to the left. I need to use `stackId` and `stackOffset=\"sign\"` together to correctly handle negative and positive stacks, otherwise the values will be incorrectly rendered. Removing the `stackOffset` will just provide normal stacking.\n\n\n**Investigating Value Summation**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how Recharts sums the values when stacking. I\'ve realized, if I stack values, the second bar starts at the end of the first. I thought the bars should start at zero, but I need to consider `barGap={0}`. It seems, by default, bars split the band. My experiments with negative values and `stackId` have clarified how the values sum. It\'s not splitting by sign automatically, and if the data is a single series, `stackOffset=\"sign\"` may be the key! I must test this.\n\n\n**Verifying Value Overlap**\n\nI\'ve confirmed my suspicions! `stackOffset=\"sign\"` correctly handles splitting positive and negative values into separate stacks originating from zero. Now, I\'m testing removing `stackId` and exploring a custom shape as a foolproof solution. If that doesn\'t work, I\'ll return to using `stackId` with a negative `barGap`. I\'m now testing overlapping bars precisely. I have an idea to test overlapping by 100% via the `barGap` attribute. I believe I have the right structure with negative values, just need to overlap them precisely! And, I\'ve just discovered `barGap` does not take a percentage string!\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 16
      nanos: 922471084
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
