  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Reviewing Percentage Assignment**\n\nI\'m now revisiting the percentage assignments. Initially, I used `update_data.js` to modify `data.ts`, specifically setting `male_pct` and `female_pct`. Now, I\'m checking if the modifications in `update_data.js` were done correctly. I want to ensure the percentage assignments are accurate and haven\'t introduced any unexpected issues, especially regarding data integrity.\n\n\n**Analyzing Data Order**\n\nI\'ve realized the data order from the API isn\'t the problem; it\'s presented in the correct age range sequence (\"0-4\", \"5-9\", etc). The issue arises with Recharts\' vertical layout, which renders the first data point at the top of the Y-axis. This means the default order naturally inverts the pyramid.\n\n\n**Confirming Array Reversal**\n\nI\'m now certain that reversing the array with `.reverse()` corrected the upside-down visualization. I tested it mentally and it confirms that the array reversal fixes the issue. If the Y axis is a category axis, the last data point would be displayed at the bottom of the axis, but it was not. I now realize the previous `.reverse()` fixed the issue the user was referring to.\n\n\n**Investigating Chart Rendering**\n\nI\'m now digging deeper into how Recharts renders the chart. I need to figure out why the user saw an upside-down pyramid. My initial thinking was array reversal, but now I\'m questioning that based on the assumed top-down rendering of SVG/HTML. The user\'s screenshot is key. I\'m trying to mentally map the data array order to the visual output and considering Recharts\' Y-axis behavior. Perhaps reversing is *not* necessary. I need to understand if the Y-axis renders data from bottom to top or top to bottom.\n\n\n**Refining Negative Values**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the `stackOffset` attribute and negative values. I\'ve realized `stackOffset=\"sign\"` isn\'t needed for Recharts\' diverging bar charts because Recharts natively handles negative values with `stackId=\"a\"` for both male and female bars, and setting male data as negative. Removing `stackOffset=\"sign\"` should resolve issues with male bars and the \"no males\" observation. I\'m also re-evaluating the role of `.reverse()` based on how Recharts renders the vertical category Y-axis. The user\'s point about the build status is unrelated.\n\n\n**Clarifying Y-Axis Orientation**\n\nI\'m now focusing on confirming the Y-axis direction in Recharts. I\'m going to remove `.reverse()` and observe the outcome, as I suspect this is unnecessary, and I\'m sure the first array entry corresponds to the top of a Recharts vertical chart, which goes to the bottom. I\'ll test it out to know for sure. Also, to fix \"no males\", I\'ll remove `stackOffset=\"sign\"` and adjust the XAxis domain, leaving the dataKey and stackId as is since they are required. I\'ll make the app simple to test this aspect first.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 36
      nanos: 449933401
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
