    thinking: "**Analyzing Chart Misalignment**\n\nOkay, I\'ve noticed the user\'s feedback about the \"ruined\" male representation in the population pyramid. Previously, I addressed this by reversing the data order and removing the `stackOffset=\"sign\"` property. I was thinking: if I use a negative value for the male bar, it should represent that side correctly, shouldn\'t it? I\'m now testing this hypothesis, and looking for data presentation errors.\n\n\n**Investigating Stack Behavior**\n\nI\'m now diving deeper into how Recharts handles diverging stacked bar charts. My initial hypothesis about negative male values seems correct; negative values draw left if the X-axis domain allows it. I\'m exploring the nuances of `stackOffset=\"sign\"` and whether it\'s essential or detrimental for this kind of visualization. I am also investigating the impact of stackId and layout on negative-valued charts, specifically around how negative and positive-valued `Bar` components should be handled.\n\n\n**Exploring StackId Dependency**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how `stackId` impacts the divergent bar chart\'s rendering. I had the thought that maybe removing the `stackId` would solve the misalignment, but I\'ve realized that in Recharts, without a shared `stackId`, the bars would be drawn side-by-side vertically, which is not what\'s intended. So, the `stackId` is actually essential for aligning the bars, even when one is negative. I have to make sure the domain encompasses the negative male percentage. I am now exploring `PopulationTab.tsx` and the `XAxis` component to address the root cause of the display issue.\n\n\n**Confirming Negative Values**\n\nI\'ve confirmed that the \'male\' data values are, in fact, negative. My code uses `-(c.male_pct || 0)` which ensures this. I am recalling the previous version\'s display issue of the male bars being upside down, and am working on reconciling the cause of the issue. The previous code structure had a domain from -10 to 10 for the X-axis, which is accurate.\n\n\n**Tracing Chart\'s Evolution**\n\nI\'m now tracing the evolution of the chart\'s rendering issues. I\'ve realized the initial problem was the chart appearing \"upside down\" and lacking male representation *before* I added `stackOffset=\"sign\"`. The core data structure, with `male_pct` and `female_pct` as positive percentages, was consistent. The removal of `stackOffset` and introduction of negative values for `male_pct` were intended to fix the misalignment, so I am going to see when these were first done. I\'m now comparing the initial and current versions of `App.tsx`, `data.ts`, `fetch_data.js`, and `generate_all.js`, to understand the sequence of changes and how I can reproduce the original issue in a controlled manner.\n\n\n**Recalling Disappearing Males**\n\nI\'ve just had a major breakthrough; I realized why the male bars initially disappeared! Before adding `stackOffset=\"sign\"`, my test data in `data.ts` used positive `male_pct` and `female_pct`. The issue was the `dataKey=\"male\"` didn\'t match the *new* calculated negative `male` values in the chart. When `male` was negative and the domain was `[-10, 10]`, without `stackOffset=\"sign\"`, Recharts was trying to stack them, meaning `female` (positive) + `male` (negative) results in *one* bar, from 0 to negative value! So, the male bar \"disappeared\" into the negative value area, stacked on top of the female bar. The correct solution, which I did not implement previously, is to ensure male and female are drawn in the intended direction.\n\n\n**Recalling Stack Offset Necessity**\n\nI\'m now tracing the effects of removing and reintroducing `stackOffset=\"sign\"`. The core issue was always *how* Recharts stacks the male and female bars, and if you *don\'t* use the `stackId`, they will always be drawn side-by-side. The initial goal was to get negative values to render correctly, and `stackOffset=\"sign\"` plays a critical role in preventing the overlap that caused the \"disappearing male\" issue. I am reviewing the version history to see what I removed!\n\n\n**Revisiting Recharts Stacking**\n\nI\'m now grappling with the user\'s feedback, focusing on the moment when `stackOffset=\"sign\"` was active and the male bars still \"disappeared\". I\'m recalling the Recharts version is 3.8.1, so it shouldn\'t have the stacking problems of version 2.x. Now, I\'m thinking about how to correctly render the pyramid. I\'m exploring the nuances of `stackId`, the data structure, and the chart domain together. I\'m exploring potential solutions, considering how to prevent overlapping and ensure that the bars are rendered as intended. My current hypothesis is that setting an X-axis domain of `[0, 100]` could be the key.\n\n\n"
